  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.2 x^2 + 0.5 z^2) / 2, drawn at half its height. Plain steps turn x by 1 - 2.2 × rate, so x zig-zags above a rate of 0.45 and swings grow above 0.91.",
    "Each step is -rate × (slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian, centred on zero, on both axes, with spread sqrt((64 - b) / (63 b)): 1 at batch 1 and exactly 0 at 64. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are steps drawn at 5 times their true length, one scale for both. The purple arrow is the step the ball takes next. The dark one is the step all 64 points would give, against the true slope.",
    "Diverged: the loss passed 5 or the ball left the drawn bowl. The ball and path are then drawn at the rim, and the Loss readout keeps the true value. Otherwise a run finishes after 40 steps.",
    "The host clock makes one update every 6 ticks (5 per second). Noise comes from a fixed sequence. A new run continues that sequence, and Reset replays it from the start."
  ]
}